Mean ± SD Bar Graph for Biofilm Assay Data

Hi! 👋

This repository contains a simple R script for turning replicate biofilm assay measurements into a Mean ± Standard Deviation (SD) bar graph.
The script was developed as a reusable example for summarising and visualising OD595 measurements from a microplate biofilm assay.

IMPORTANT:
The example data and sample names used in this repository are fictional and are only provided to demonstrate how the code works.


WHAT DOES THE SCRIPT DO?
The script:

1. Imports data from an Excel file.
2. Removes unwanted rows such as blank wells.
3. Selects the concentrations used in the analysis.
4. Calculates the mean OD595.
5. Calculates the standard deviation (SD).
6. Creates a grouped bar graph.
7. Adds SD error bars.
8. Creates a separate panel for each bacterial strain.
9. Allows each panel to have its own y-axis scale.
10. Saves the graph as a high-resolution PNG file.
11. Saves the calculated mean and SD as an Excel file.


EXAMPLE EXPERIMENT
The example in this repository uses fictional data representing an experiment involving:

Organism:
Escherichia coli

Example strains:
EC01
EC02
EC03
EC04
EC05
EC06
EC07

Treatments:
Manuka
Kelulut

Concentrations:
3.125%
6.25%
12.5%
25%
50%
100%

Measurement:
OD595


REQUIRED DATA FORMAT
The input Excel file should contain the following columns:
Strain
Treatment
Concentration
Replicate
OD595

Each row represents one replicate measurement.
Example:
Strain: EC01
Treatment: Manuka
Concentration: 3.125
Replicate: 1
OD595: 0.54

Strain: EC01
Treatment: Manuka
Concentration: 3.125
Replicate: 2
OD595: 0.57

Strain: EC01
Treatment: Kelulut
Concentration: 3.125
Replicate: 1
OD595: 0.48

Strain: EC01
Treatment: Kelulut
Concentration: 3.125
Replicate: 2
OD595: 0.46

REQUIREMENTS
You will need:
- R
- RStudio

The script uses the following R packages:
readxl
dplyr
ggplot2
ggh4x
writexl

If you do not already have these packages, install them using:
install.packages(c("readxl", "dplyr", "ggplot2", "ggh4x", "writexl"))

HOW TO USE THE SCRIPT
STEP 1: Prepare your Excel file.
Organise your data using the required column names:

Strain
Treatment
Concentration
Replicate
OD595


STEP 2: Open the R script.
Open the R script in RStudio.

STEP 3: Change the input file.
Find the section where the Excel file is imported.
Replace the example file name with the location of your own Excel file.
For example:

data <- read_excel("your_data_file.xlsx")


STEP 4: Check your column names.
Run:

names(data)

This allows you to check whether R is reading your column names correctly.

STEP 5: Run the script.
Run the code in RStudio.
The script will calculate the Mean OD595 and SD OD595 for each combination of:
Strain × Treatment × Concentration


UNDERSTANDING THE GRAPH

BAR HEIGHT
The height of each bar represents the mean OD595 of the replicate measurements.

ERROR BARS
The error bars represent standard deviation (SD).
The error bars are calculated as:

Mean - SD

and

Mean + SD


COLOURS
Different colours represent different treatment groups.

In the example:
Manuka = orange
Kelulut = purple


PANELS
Each bacterial strain is displayed in its own panel.
The y-axis is allowed to vary between panels using a free y-axis scale.
This can make patterns easier to see when different strains have substantially different OD595 values.


HOW ARE THE MEAN AND SD CALCULATED?

For each Strain × Treatment × Concentration combination, the script calculates:

Mean OD595
and
Standard Deviation (SD)

Missing values are ignored during the calculation.
The resulting summary data contain:
Strain
Treatment
Concentration
Mean_OD595
SD_OD595


OUTPUT FILES
After running the script, two files are produced.

1. MBIC_BarChart.png

This is the final bar graph.
The graph is saved as a high-resolution PNG image at 600 dpi.


2. MBIC_Summary_Table.xlsx

This Excel file contains the calculated summary values, including:
Strain
Treatment
Concentration
Mean_OD595
SD_OD595

This file can be used to check the calculations or for further analysis.


IF YOUR DATA ARE DIFFERENT

That's completely fine!

This script is a template, so your dataset may need some adjustments.

For example, your data may have:

- Different column names
- Different treatment names
- Different concentrations
- Different numbers of replicates
- Additional control groups
- More or fewer strains

If the structure of your data is different, you may need to modify the filtering, grouping, or plotting sections of the code.

PROBLEM: The graph does not look right.
Check the values in your Treatment and Concentration columns.
You can use:

unique(data$Treatment)

and:

unique(data$Concentration)

This can help identify spelling differences or unexpected values.
For example:

Manuka
manuka
Manuka Honey

would be treated as three different treatment groups by R.


IMPORTANT NOTE
This script is mainly intended as a data visualisation and summary template.
Mean ± SD describes the observed variation between replicate measurements. It does not by itself constitute a statistical significance test.
Make sure that the statistical analysis used for your research question is appropriate for your experimental design.

⚠️ If your data look different

Don't panic! 😂

This code was written for a particular experimental data structure, so it may not work immediately with every dataset.

If your data are organised differently, you may need to modify the data-processing section.

💡 Troubleshooting tip

If you get stuck, Microsoft 365 Copilot can also be helpful for troubleshooting R code.

You can give Copilot:

-Your R code
-A small example of your dataset
-The error message
-Snapshot of your screen (window+shift+s)
